WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.920
One of Game of Thrones' longest-running bad
guys remained a mystery to readers and viewers

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.940
until the HBO series' eighth and final season:
the Night King, the ancient creature who created

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.000
and led the formidable Army of the Dead.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:16.180
We still don't know much about the White Walker
king, but from behind-the-scenes tidbits to

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:19.330
fan theories, here's the untold truth of this
menacing bad guy.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.859
Slovakian actor Vladimir Furdík might be
the most recognizable face of the Night King

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:28.949
at this point, but the truth is, he only started
playing the character in the show's sixth

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:33.159
season, prior to that, the role was played
by Richard Brake, best known for films like

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.740
Batman Begins or Kingsman: The Secret Service.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.220
Brake and Furdík actually overlapped: while
Brake was playing the Night King, Furdík

00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:44.110
played the high-ranking White Walker that
Jon Snow shatters to pieces with his Valyrian

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:44.760
steel sword.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.940
The transition between the two actors was
so seamless that many may not have noticed,

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.799
though some fans found Brake's appearance
even more frightening.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.040
Furdík was an indispensable part of the Thrones
team long before he stepped into the Night

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.040
King's frozen shoes.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.359
He worked on everything from the fight scene
at the Tower of Joy as well as performing

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.409
and working on everything from high falls
to intense sword fights.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.740
When it came time to recast the Night King,
there was no better choice than Furdík, who

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.159
knew the show's action set pieces inside and
out.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.960
Though the Night King's human name is never
revealed and audiences still don't have a

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.509
firm handle on his personal history, the show
did confirm during season 6 that he was once

00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:21.000
a human.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.100
He was a member of the group known as the
First Men, and he was used as a pawn by the

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.240
Children of the Forest.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:31.100
The Children of the Forest made a home in
Westeros long before humans existed, and were

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.880
settled happily in the Seven Kingdoms until
they were forced to deal with the First Men.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.280
After the First Men crossed from Essos into
what is now Dorne, they came into conflict

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.040
with the Children.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.820
In the end, the Children were responsible
for creating the Night King when they took

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:47.340
one of the First Men hostage and plunged dragonglass
into his heart.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.289
In doing so, they inadvertently created ultimate
evil by making the first ever White Walker

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:54.840
as a means of defending themselves against
men.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:55.880
"It was you.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.520
You made the White Walkers."

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.579
"We were at war.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.640
We were being slaughtered."

00:02:03.679 --> 00:02:07.430
The creation of the Night King turned out
to cause more problems than it solved; after

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:11.290
all, an undead monster who can raise entire
armies of zombies is bound to cause a few

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:11.820
issues.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.280
He turned against the Children of the Forest,
and they were forced to fight back.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.620
When the First Men and Children of the Forest
found themselves under attack by the White

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.540
Walkers, the two warring factions were forced
to team up and work together to save both;

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.340
ultimately, the First Men triumphed at the
Battle of the Dawn.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.340
After the war was over, the First Men worked
with giants and the Children to build the

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:30.840
Wall.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.760
White Walkers have always been a big part
of Thrones' mythology.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.100
"Correct.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.420
A student of history, are you?"

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.060
After all, the show's pilot opened with White
Walkers attacking members of the Night's Watch.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.860
Every now and then, during the show's earlier
seasons, viewers would be reminded that no

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.840
matter what else was going on in Westeros,
the White Walkers were still out there and

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.680
ready to attack.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.140
You might think the Night King has been around
for the whole show, but he actually didn't

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.060
make his first appearance until season four's
"Oathkeeper."

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.300
That's the episode which finally reveals the
fate of the many babies viewers saw offered

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.380
as sacrifices.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.740
During the audience's first-ever glimpse of
the Night King, he turns a baby into a White

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:09.240
Walker.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.420
Throughout the series, the show has diverged
dramatically from the books.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.909
Author George R.R. Martin, for his part, has
a lot to say about the Night King's history,

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:22.720
and especially wants to make sure people don't
confuse two figures he sees as especially

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.050
distinct: the Night's King and the Night King.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:26.560
"I'm a bit confused."

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.180
The Night King audiences know and fear isn't
the same as the Night's King; as Martin has

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.600
explained, the Night's King is a legendary
figure like Bran the Builder.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.040
In the books, the Night's King was an early
commander of the Night's Watch.

00:03:38.049 --> 00:03:42.349
Thanks to an ill-fated love affair, he turned
against the Watch, causing the King Beyond

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:45.320
the Wall and the King in the North to band
together and take him down.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:49.640
This little distinction might not seem important,
but ultimately, it serves to distinguish two

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.160
similarly named but radically different characters.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.860
As the Night King makes his way through the
icy North and builds up his army, it remains

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.240
a mystery as to what he wants beyond destruction,
death, and to move beyond the Wall.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:06.069
In spite of that inscrutability, Furdík has
made some executive decisions about his character

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:06.700
motivations.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.620
Throughout the show, hints are dropped about
why the Night King is who he is and does what

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:11.120
he does.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.799
In various interviews, Furdík has said that
he thinks that the Night King wants revenge

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.560
for being the Night King in the first place.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:21.300
As the result of years of conflict and mistakes,
the Night King became the Night King against

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.120
his will and has spent the rest of his existence
trying to fight back against those who wronged

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:25.620
him.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.760
The show also gave viewers one significant
piece of evidence about his motives: in the

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.410
final season's second episode, "A Knight of
the Seven Kingdoms," Bran Stark reveals the

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.170
Night King will come for him to accomplish
his true purpose:

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:38.480
"An endless night.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.960
He wants to erase this world.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.840
And I am its memory."

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.960
"That's what death is, isn't it?"

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.860
One of the longest-running theories among
Thrones fans argues that, upon Bran's transformation

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.910
into the all-knowing Three-Eyed Raven, Bran
Stark had actually become a part of the Night

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:57.480
King himself.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.380
Throughout Bran's training with the previous
Raven, he found himself able to "warg" back

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.260
in time and see previous events.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:07.240
During one visit to the Army of the Dead,
he ended up branded with the Night King's

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.460
mark, a twist that left some viewers looking
for deeper meaning.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:11.920
"His mark is on me.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.040
He always knows where I am."

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.670
One of the most popular theories circulating
on this topic suggested that Bran could have

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:24.540
warged to the past to prevent the Children
of the Forest from creating the Night King,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.830
but in doing so, he became trapped within
the Night King in some way.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.380
An intriguing theory, but now that the Night
King has come for Bran and gotten himself

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.040
murdered in the process, we know it's one
that was obviously off the mark.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.680
In trying to figure out the Night King's true
origins, numerous fans have wondered if he

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.700
might have come from a noble family like the
Lannisters, Boltons, or Tyrells.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.300
As far as ancient houses go, Targaryen is
certainly one of the oldest around, and many

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.960
viewers theorized that the Night King might
be descended from this royal line.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:56.420
After all, he can ride a dragon, and he likes
arranging carnage in a spiral pattern.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:01.060
However, the Targaryen connection is pretty
easy to debunk considering the timeline doesn't

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:05.080
really allow for it; that theory, while interesting,
has been moot for some time.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.520
The more appealing theory points to the Night
King being a Stark, especially since fans

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.150
know for sure that the Night King was one
of the First Men, who are the Stark's ancestors.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.320
It's possible that a Stark ancestor was the
man who eventually became the Night King,

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.790
which explains why he always seems to hunt
members of the Stark family in particular,

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.260
they're his family, but they ultimately turned
on him.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:28.360
The showrunners for Game of Thrones have clearly
had long-awaited plans that are just now coming

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:29.040
to fruition.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:32.920
The fact that it was Arya Stark, and not Jon
Snow or someone else, who would eventually

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.640
kill the Night King was something the showrunners
had planned for several years.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:40.240
As far as the Night King goes, they've been
carefully considering this character for a

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:40.780
long time.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.500
However, that doesn't mean they've shared
those plans with the actor who actually plays

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:45.180
the Night King.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.580
In an interview with The Hollywood Reporter,
Furdík revealed that the directors and showrunners

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.540
were as silent as the famous character:

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.280
"No, they didn't give me [much].

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.850
There were a couple of discussions with the
directors, but nothing particular about what

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:57.780
he was supposed to do."

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.840
Furdik indicated that since he got the sense
that they knew the character inside and out,

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.930
he didn't necessarily need the same information.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:08.120
No matter what you might think of this approach,
it certainly didn't affect the storytelling

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.380
or Furdík's beautifully creepy performance.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:15.360
Fire has long been one of the most important
weapons deployed against wights, the zombies

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.060
created by the White Walkers.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.660
A body can be reanimated if it isn't incinerated,
which is why the burial rites for the Night's

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.110
Watch involve cremation.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.470
Luckily, Daenerys Targaryen and her team have
a vital resource that can help fight against

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:32.660
wights and White Walkers: dragons, which can
soar high above the battle unleashing dragonfire

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:33.760
on any enemies below.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.800
The Night King obviously has an affinity for
these beasts, considering he took down one

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.680
of Daenerys' dragons during the show's seventh
season.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:44.800
But during "The Long Night," it becomes clear
that even a living dragon wouldn't be an effective

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:45.800
weapon against him.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:50.220
Daenerys tries her tried and true burning
trick, but the Night King just stands there

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.360
staring, completely immune to dragonfire.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:54.220
"It's embarrassing, really."

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.240
As the Night King and his army advanced on
Winterfell, characters wondered how they'd

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.240
possibly prevail against an army that could
simply continue multiplying throughout the

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:03.740
night.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.640
The battle itself was relentless, pitting
the North and their allies against a massive

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:08.720
army of the undead.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:12.860
As the Night King made his way to the Godswood
to find Bran, it seemed as if the end was

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.060
night and the Army of the Dead would finally
triumph.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:19.180
Even though the Northern squad knew they could
destroy the entire army if they only took

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.520
out the Night King, the chances of them doing
that seemed slim at best.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:26.700
Thankfully, there was one fearless force running
around that most viewers forgot about halfway

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:31.340
through the episode, specifically Arya, armed
with high jumping skills and a dagger of Valyrian

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:31.960
steel.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.219
Thanks to years of training as an assassin
and the element of surprise, Arya was able

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:39.339
to finally put an end to the Night King and
the Army of the Dead.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.740
"What do we say to the God of Death?"

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:47.880
"Not today."

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.780
Even as a professional stuntman, Furdík wasn't
immune to the grueling schedule of filming

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.019
"The Long Night."

00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:58.839
Filming the episode required a 55-day shoot
over several nights in Belfast that many members

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.720
of the cast and crew bluntly described as
"miserable."

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.500
When speaking to The Hollywood Reporter, Furdík
looked back on the shoot as extremely tough:

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:08.580
"This was one of the hardest jobs of my life."

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.300
It makes sense.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.490
Besides performing as the Night King, he also
worked with nearly every other actor to rehearse

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:16.480
the fight scenes and choreography.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.810
One of the toughest scenes to shoot, understandably,
was Furdík's final scene with Maisie Williams

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.060
as Arya kills him.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.660
"It was a very emotional day and night...It
was very, very difficult.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:26.920
We are very good friends.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:27.780
We know each other.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.740
It wasn't easy for me to [pretend to] hurt
her.

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:33.520
If you make a bad move, if you don't grab
her well, she could have an injury.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:35.760
So I was under pressure and she was under
pressure.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.440
It was not an easy day."

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:41.360
Furdík worked with Williams on her fight
with Gwendoline Christie's Brienne, so the

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.660
two have a strong combat history together;
clearly, it paid off with the perfect shot.

